{% if host == 'gitlab' %}# Issue tracker: GitLab

Issues, specs and tickets for this repository live in its GitLab Issues. Read
and write them with the `glab` CLI. (This file is what `setup-matt-pocock-skills`
would create; it is pre-filled, so do not run that skill unless you change
trackers. Original: mattpocock/skills, MIT.)

## Before you write anything

Read the repository you are writing into: its `AGENTS.md`, its `CONTRIBUTING.md`,
and the issue and merge request templates in `.gitlab/issue_templates/` and
`.gitlab/merge_request_templates/`.

Writing through `glab` or the API does not exempt you from the template. It only
means nothing fills it in for you, so you read it and follow it yourself:
`glab issue create --template Task` starts from it.

**This file describes your repository's conventions.** They came from the
template, and they are yours to change — a team that settles on a different
shape edits this file and the templates together, and is not in violation of
anything. Writing into someone else's repository, use theirs.

## Four kinds of issue

Name the kind, and the template you write it in, before you write the description.
A defect is a `bug`, whoever finds it: a person who reports it, or an agent
that reproduced it and can say what the fix must do. A feature request is an
`enhancement`. Any other issue takes its kind from its result. When the result
is a decision, an answer or a map, the issue is a record (`wayfinder:research`,
`wayfinder:grilling`, `wayfinder:map`, or `spec`); when the result changes code
or documents, it is a `task`. When work
waits on a decision, open the record first, and open the task after the
decision, from it. Every issue carries a
kind label: after `glab issue create`, read it back with `glab issue view <n>`
and add the label if it is missing.

The templates are in `.gitlab/issue_templates/`. Each ends in a `/label` quick
action that adds its label when the issue is created; written by hand, keep the
headings and add the label yourself.

| Kind | Template and label | Shape |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A defect, reported by anyone; a feature request | Bug (`bug`), Feature (`enhancement`) | The template's headings: what happened, steps, expected, environment, logs; or what you are blocked on, how you would like it, what you tried. A `bug` made `ready-for-agent` gains `## Acceptance criteria` (and `## Not building` when it helps) below the template's headings, in the same issue: `/implement` reads them |
| A task, one thing to build | Task (`task`) | `## Parent`, `## What to build`, `## Acceptance criteria`, `## Not building`, `## Blocked by`; while open, decisions go under `## Decisions` (dated) and the result under `## Outcome` |
| The end of the project, or handing it over | End-or-hand-over (`task`) | The steps as a checklist (data, keys, users, package, image and service, repository), `## Not needed` for those that do not apply, and three questions: what went well, what the agent kept getting wrong, which rule or check to add |
| A record: research, a decision, a map, a spec | blank issue; the kind is a label: `wayfinder:map`, `wayfinder:research`, `wayfinder:grilling`, or `spec`, plus the triage label while open | `## Question`, `## Findings`, `## Decision`, `## Used by` for research; the map and the spec keep their own shape |

The body is the record and stays canonical; comments are conversation, and
a decision made in a comment is moved into the body. Add one triage label
from [triage-labels.md](triage-labels.md): `needs-triage` by default,
`ready-for-agent` when the issue is fully specified.

## What a record has to carry

Whatever the format, these survive:

- **An issue**: the problem, why it is worth solving, what done looks like, the
  decisions taken and their reasons, and the outcome on closing.
- **A merge request**: the final change, why this approach, the evidence and its
  limits, and the related issue.

When a discussion or a review changes the conclusion, update the body. Keep the
earlier reasoning and the reason it was corrected; a record that shows only the
final answer cannot be argued with.

Distinguish **completed** from **cancelled**, **superseded**, and **moved to a
follow-up** — closing is not one state. An item still owed when an issue closes
goes into an open follow-up issue that the Outcome links, where a read of open
issues finds it. Distinguish an automated check from an agent review from a
person's own use of the thing from something not run at all.

A filled-in section is not value and an edited file is not completion. Judge by
what the record says happened, not by whether the headings are all present.

Fields, labels and syntax that automation depends on are followed exactly:
`Closes` auto-closes, a label drives triage queries, and a template heading is
read by tooling. Precision there is not style.

### Public records

Write issues and merge requests as public records, which they are on a public
project: they record the product, not the computer it was made on. An
environment detail goes in when a result depends on it or it limits what was
observed, written as the condition without the person: "a checkout path
containing a space", "git 2.36", "on one machine". Home paths, account and
folder names and credential values stay out, or are replaced by what matters
about them: `~/...` for a path in the home folder, or the condition the path
shows ("a path containing a space"). A pasted error message is the usual way in,
since tools print full home paths: read it before it goes in. Local housekeeping
the result does not depend on stays out too: what was installed, registered or
removed on the person's computer. Keep decisions, dates, numbers, links,
acceptance criteria and checkbox states. A description's earlier versions can
stay readable in its history: text taken out later is not gone because the
description no longer shows it.

## Conventions

- **Another repository's issue or merge request**: A reference to another repository's issue or merge request is `group/project#N` for an issue and `group/project!N` for a merge request; a bare `#N`, or `plinth #N`, links to this repository's own `#N`. That holds in descriptions, comments and the closing line alike.
- **Create an issue**: `glab issue create --title "..." --description "..." --label "..."`, or `--template Task`. Multi-line bodies via `--description-file`.
- **Title**: one sentence saying what the issue does, such as "Make the engine spec English canonical with a Korean translation". Not a merge request's `type(scope):` form: nothing reads an issue title but people scanning a list. A record may start with its kind, such as `Research:`.
- **A ticket from a spec or plan**: keep its id (such as `T6.16`) and its original title in `## Parent`, beside the parent's `#N`, so a search for the id still finds it.
- **Read an issue**: `glab issue view <number> --comments`
- **List issues**: `glab issue list -O json --jq '[.[] | {iid, title, description, labels}]'` (filter with `--label`; `--closed` or `--all` for more than the open ones)
- **Comment**: `glab issue note <number> --message "..."`
- **Labels**: `glab issue update <number> --label "..."` / `--unlabel "..."`
- **Close**: `glab issue note <number> --message "..."`, then `glab issue close <number>`

`glab` reads the project from `git remote -v`, so run it inside the clone.

